WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:09.005
As an Iranian refugee
who grew up in rural east Tennessee,

00:00:09.047 --> 00:00:10.715
and someone who studied,

00:00:10.715 --> 00:00:13.760
lived and worked in over 30 countries,

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.807
I thought I knew how to navigate
across cultures and languages.

00:00:19.516 --> 00:00:20.684
I was wrong.

00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:23.770
There I was, day one of business school,

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.857
getting my bearings
and hearing an alien language.

00:00:28.149 --> 00:00:31.987
It wasn't English, French,
or my native Persian.

00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:34.823
It was a bouillabaisse of languages,

00:00:34.823 --> 00:00:39.744
jargon from across
the organizational multiverse:

00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:41.204
the corporate world,

00:00:41.204 --> 00:00:42.914
the nonprofit sphere,

00:00:42.956 --> 00:00:44.624
international development

00:00:44.624 --> 00:00:46.293
and the public sector.

00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:48.837
It was weird.

00:00:49.921 --> 00:00:53.758
In business school, I thought
I'd learn about leadership styles.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.345
What I found were leadership languages.

00:00:57.387 --> 00:01:01.474
And I learned that by becoming
conversant in these languages

00:01:01.516 --> 00:01:06.104
that I could communicate with
and contribute to all of these worlds.

00:01:07.022 --> 00:01:11.234
So how does one become multilingual?

00:01:11.735 --> 00:01:13.028
Here's my story.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.324
In college, I went to West Africa
to study microfinance

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:19.534
and economic empowerment.

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:22.287
Seeing a large public health need,

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:25.123
I cofounded and led a nonprofit

00:01:25.165 --> 00:01:27.667
collecting medical supplies and money.

00:01:28.668 --> 00:01:31.671
We saw what collective action could yield,

00:01:31.713 --> 00:01:35.008
and we were creative with our advocacy
and our fundraising,

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.136
with campaigns like "Band Aids for Benin."

00:01:39.346 --> 00:01:42.849
Along the way, I studied
different leadership styles:

00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:44.517
autocratic, democratic,

00:01:44.559 --> 00:01:49.898
laissez faire, servant, collaborative,
transformational and more.

00:01:50.565 --> 00:01:53.318
I read books, attended trainings

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.364
and I even had the privilege
to study with some of the greats

00:01:57.405 --> 00:02:02.077
and to work with General Colin
Powell and Dr. Maya Angelou.

00:02:03.411 --> 00:02:05.080
After all of this,

00:02:05.121 --> 00:02:10.251
I became convinced that there's no
best type of leadership.

00:02:10.794 --> 00:02:15.131
So many of us just default
to one style or another,

00:02:15.173 --> 00:02:17.967
similar to how we're born
with our native tongue.

00:02:19.052 --> 00:02:23.056
But this default style
doesn't work in every situation.

00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:27.227
Many leaders obsess over leadership style

00:02:27.227 --> 00:02:30.772
when really they just need
to get over themselves.

00:02:31.064 --> 00:02:32.899
(Laughter)

00:02:32.899 --> 00:02:36.903
(Applause)

00:02:36.945 --> 00:02:39.364
Sorry, leader, it's not about you.

00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:40.782
(Laughter)

00:02:40.824 --> 00:02:46.037
What's more useful is to adapt
and personalize your leadership

00:02:46.037 --> 00:02:51.334
to be the leader the situation
and the people involved need.

00:02:52.127 --> 00:02:56.214
It's not so much style as language,

00:02:56.214 --> 00:03:02.137
the living vernacular of each discrete
community and culture.

00:03:03.388 --> 00:03:07.350
The successful leader will aspire
to be multilingual,

00:03:07.392 --> 00:03:12.063
to speak fluently with all groups
and to translate among them.

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:17.235
In 2012, while working
at the Omidyar Network,

00:03:17.235 --> 00:03:20.447
I was one of the sponsors
of a two-year study

00:03:20.488 --> 00:03:23.324
of 12 impact investing funds.

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:26.578
One of the "ahas" of this project

00:03:26.619 --> 00:03:32.542
was that the most successful funds
were those led by multilingual leaders,

00:03:32.584 --> 00:03:37.422
people that could communicate across
the corporate, nonprofit, philanthropic

00:03:37.464 --> 00:03:39.799
and public policy perspectives.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.343
These leaders also made sure

00:03:42.385 --> 00:03:45.221
that others on their team
were multilingual.

00:03:46.931 --> 00:03:49.726
This came to me as a bit of a surprise.

00:03:49.726 --> 00:03:53.813
I expected the secret sauce to be
in sophisticated financial modeling

00:03:53.855 --> 00:03:56.691
or some other type of complex analysis.

00:03:57.984 --> 00:03:59.778
According to this study,

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:03.281
the multilingual leader
will describe their work

00:04:03.323 --> 00:04:08.036
as being unremittingly
financially driven -- business speak,

00:04:08.036 --> 00:04:12.499
moving the needle on social
and environmental challenges --

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.543
nonprofit and philanthropic speak,

00:04:15.543 --> 00:04:19.798
while addressing systemic
market failures --

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:21.549
public policy speak.

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:23.927
The study further found

00:04:23.968 --> 00:04:27.972
the need to be conversant
into four industry languages.

00:04:27.972 --> 00:04:32.852
Those are finance, nonprofit, government
and international development.

00:04:33.561 --> 00:04:37.106
The multilingual leader

00:04:37.148 --> 00:04:39.484
was something that seemed
very familiar to me.

00:04:39.526 --> 00:04:41.903
This really rang true

00:04:41.945 --> 00:04:47.367
based on my own experience
of patchworks across these sectors,

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:52.872
and it took me back to that first
weird day in business school.

00:04:54.499 --> 00:04:58.253
Now that we've described the how,
let us turn to the what.

00:04:58.253 --> 00:05:00.338
Where should leaders focus?

00:05:00.338 --> 00:05:04.843
I will modestly propose three topics
that cut across issues,

00:05:04.884 --> 00:05:07.387
and together we'll double-click
on one of them.

00:05:08.388 --> 00:05:11.975
Data, climate, power.

00:05:12.642 --> 00:05:17.522
How each of these sectors measure
the impact and even the timing,

00:05:17.522 --> 00:05:21.067
the tempo and the tools
that they use for each

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:22.944
could not be more different.

00:05:23.903 --> 00:05:28.241
Say you're the private sector at one end.

00:05:28.241 --> 00:05:30.618
You have accounting on a daily, a weekly,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.204
a monthly and quarterly increments.

00:05:33.246 --> 00:05:34.497
The public sector.

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:37.750
You're working on multi-year
election cycles.

00:05:37.792 --> 00:05:39.711
The nonprofit and philanthropic sector,

00:05:39.711 --> 00:05:44.382
well, you may give yourself 15 years
or even a generation to reach a goal.

00:05:45.383 --> 00:05:48.094
Yet all of these sectors
must come together

00:05:48.136 --> 00:05:49.804
to reach these outcomes.

00:05:50.805 --> 00:05:52.265
To make it real,

00:05:52.265 --> 00:05:55.894
let's look together about how
these sectors can come together

00:05:55.894 --> 00:05:57.478
to address power

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.772
with the focus on gender equality.

00:06:00.899 --> 00:06:02.901
Say you're a public sector leader.

00:06:02.942 --> 00:06:08.448
You have the unique ability to create
equity, a level playing field,

00:06:08.448 --> 00:06:11.200
so everyone has a fair shot.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.120
You can create regulations and incentives

00:06:14.162 --> 00:06:17.290
to increase the use of gender data.

00:06:17.332 --> 00:06:21.961
You can set goals for women
on boards and in public office.

00:06:22.003 --> 00:06:26.507
You can practice gender budgeting
and you can mandate paid care.

00:06:28.927 --> 00:06:32.096
Say you're a leader
in the business sector.

00:06:32.138 --> 00:06:36.100
You can design products and services
that unleash the economic power

00:06:36.142 --> 00:06:37.977
of the female customer.

00:06:38.019 --> 00:06:41.439
You can fortify and diversify
your supply chain

00:06:41.481 --> 00:06:43.441
with women-owned businesses,

00:06:43.441 --> 00:06:46.653
and you can invest
in your female talent pipeline

00:06:46.653 --> 00:06:49.280
with pay equity
and leadership opportunities

00:06:49.322 --> 00:06:52.742
so that your corporate decisions
are more inclusive.

00:06:53.993 --> 00:06:59.332
Or maybe you work in philanthropy
or international development.

00:07:00.458 --> 00:07:04.796
You can help remove the barriers,
whether formal or informal,

00:07:04.837 --> 00:07:07.757
that influence how power is distributed.

00:07:08.633 --> 00:07:11.010
You can fund the research and training

00:07:11.052 --> 00:07:14.931
that both the public and the private
sector need to do their part.

00:07:14.973 --> 00:07:17.684
You can fund campaigns
and grassroots efforts

00:07:17.725 --> 00:07:21.604
that influence social and cultural norms.

00:07:22.146 --> 00:07:26.985
And you can help get women
in decision-making roles

00:07:26.985 --> 00:07:30.154
in their households, in their communities,

00:07:30.196 --> 00:07:33.408
in their countries and in economies.

00:07:35.118 --> 00:07:38.621
The multilingual leader can be conversant

00:07:38.663 --> 00:07:42.208
and use the languages
of all of these sectors

00:07:42.208 --> 00:07:45.003
to identify the root causes,

00:07:45.003 --> 00:07:47.213
and then collaborate together

00:07:47.255 --> 00:07:51.217
to find the solutions
to these complex issues.

00:07:52.176 --> 00:07:58.141
So here is over 20 years of experience
distilled into one sentence.

00:07:58.641 --> 00:08:02.812
Don't fret over your leadership styles,

00:08:03.146 --> 00:08:06.149
focus on your leadership languages.

00:08:06.858 --> 00:08:08.693
So over to you, leader.

00:08:08.735 --> 00:08:11.612
Which languages
are you going to learn next?

00:08:12.113 --> 00:08:13.239
Thank you.

00:08:13.281 --> 00:08:15.491
(Applause)

